     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hello everyone,<div><br></div><div>I&#39;m working on some analysis in which I wish to acquire amplitude values for a grid of time and amplitude coordinates. The simplest way to do this would be to store the result of &#39;s<span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\">pectro&#39; as an object </span>and use the $time and $frequency to acquire $amp values. However, this approach is linked to the window length (FFT = wl = 512) and it generates a very large amount of data. </div><div><br></div><div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> som1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> readWave</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;olfersii.wav&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br></span></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sonogram1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> spectro</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">som1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> wl</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">512</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> tlim</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> flim</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">15</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> plot</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">F</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> length</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sonogram1$time</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">344</span></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> length</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sonogram1$freq</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">175</span></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> length</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sonogram1$amp</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">60200</span></div><div><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></div></code></div><br>What I was hoping to do would be to simplify the grid of coordinates without the need to change FFT values (which would incur in spectrogram changes). </div><div>By using the current window length, the grid of coordinates looks like this (fig &#39;original grid.jpeg&#39; attached):</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><div>plot(x= c(0, 4), y=c(0,15))</div><div>abline(v=sonogram1$time, h=sonogram1$freq)</div></div></code></div><div><br></div><div>Whereas I would like the grid to be like this (fig &#39;intended grid.jpeg&#39; attached):</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><div>plot(x= c(0, 4), y=c(0,15))</div><div>abline(v=seq(0, 4, length.out = 100))</div><div>abline(h=seq(0, 15, length.out = 80))</div><div><br></div></div></code></div><br>In which I would acquire a amplitude value for each set of time and frequency coordinates in the grid. </div><div><br></div><div>I gave a look in seewave documentation and searched for similar approaches, but I couldn&#39;t sort a way of doing it. </div><div><br></div><div>I&#39;ll appreciate any help.</div><div><br></div><div>Pedro</div><br><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div></div></div>"
